=== PART A: argv[1] -> i32 (main's conversion) ===

Disassembly of main confirms it exactly (0x4037de..0x4037fc):
    mov 0x8(%rbp),%rdi      ; argv[1]
    mov $0xa,%edx           ; base = 10
    xor %esi,%esi           ; endptr = NULL
    call strtol@plt         ; returns long (64-bit)
    mov %eax,%ebx           ; <-- takes only the LOW 32 BITS: plain truncation, NO clamping to int
So: num = (i32) glibc_strtol_base10(argv[1])   with wrapping truncation of the 64-bit result.

Rust-ready emulation of glibc strtol(s, NULL, 10) in the default "C" locale:

    fn glibc_strtol_base10(bytes: &[u8]) -> i64 {
        let mut i = 0usize;
        // 1. skip leading whitespace: exactly isspace() in the C locale
        //    = b' ', b'\t' (0x09), b'\n' (0x0a), 0x0b (VT), 0x0c (FF), b'\r' (0x0d)
        while i < bytes.len()
            && matches!(bytes[i], b' ' | 0x09 | 0x0a | 0x0b | 0x0c | 0x0d) { i += 1; }
        // 2. at most ONE optional sign
        let mut neg = false;
        if i < bytes.len() && (bytes[i] == b'+' || bytes[i] == b'-') {
            neg = bytes[i] == b'-';
            i += 1;
        }
        // 3. longest run of ASCII digits b'0'..=b'9' (base 10: no 0x/0b prefix handling,
        //    no '_' separators, no thousands grouping, no non-ASCII digits)
        let start = i;
        let mut acc: u128 = 0;
        let mut overflow = false;
        let limit: u128 = if neg { 9223372036854775808u128 }   // |i64::MIN|
                          else   { 9223372036854775807u128 };  // i64::MAX
        while i < bytes.len() && bytes[i].is_ascii_digit() {
            let d = (bytes[i] - b'0') as u128;
            if !overflow {
                acc = acc * 10 + d;
                if acc > limit { overflow = true; }
            }
            i += 1;
        }
        // 4. no digits consumed at all -> 0 (errno EINVAL, value 0)
        if i == start { return 0; }
        // 5. out of long range -> SATURATE to LONG_MAX / LONG_MIN (errno ERANGE)
        if overflow { return if neg { i64::MIN } else { i64::MAX }; }
        // 6. in range
        if neg { (acc as i128).wrapping_neg() as i64 } else { acc as i64 }
        //   (acc == 2^63 with neg is exactly i64::MIN and is NOT overflow)
    }

    fn argv_to_num(arg: &str) -> i32 {
        glibc_strtol_base10(arg.as_bytes()) as i32   // `as i32` = wrapping truncation, mod 2^32
    }

Key consequences (all observed):
  * SATURATE at the 64-bit long boundary, then TRUNCATE to 32 bits.
    "2147483648"            -> long 2147483648      -> i32 -2147483648  (wraparound)
    "2147483649"            -> long 2147483649      -> i32 -2147483647
    "4294967296" (2^32)     -> long 4294967296      -> i32 0
    "4294967297"            ->                       -> i32 1
    "9999999999999999999"   -> saturates to i64::MAX -> low 32 bits 0xFFFFFFFF -> i32 -1
    "-9999999999999999999"  -> saturates to i64::MIN (0x8000_0000_0000_0000) -> i32 0
    "-9223372036854775807"  -> 0x8000000000000001    -> i32 1
  * Leading whitespace of any of the 6 C-locale kinds is skipped; a UTF-8 NBSP (C2 A0) is NOT.
  * Trailing garbage is simply ignored: "12abc"->12, "1e3"->1, "3.9"->3, "1 2"->1, "21st"->21,
    "1,234"->1, "1_000"->1, "0x1A"->0 (stops at 'x'), "0b11"->0.
  * No digits at all -> 0: "", "abc", "abc12", "--5", "-+5", "- 13", " -  13", "+", "-", ".5", "٤٢".
  * Leading zeros are irrelevant (base is fixed 10): "007"->7, "-011"->-11,
    32-digit zero-padded "...021"->21. Zero padding does NOT prevent overflow saturation.

=== PART B: ordinalize(i32) ===

Disassembly of 0x40681c confirms: std::to_string(int), then signed C remainders, then string concat.
Literals: 0x431401="th", 0x431404="st", 0x431407="nd", 0x43140a="rd".

    fn ordinalize(num: i32) -> String {
        let s = num.to_string();       // == std::to_string(int); i32::MIN -> "-2147483648"
        let m100 = num % 100;          // C truncating remainder; Rust `%` on i32 is identical
        if m100 == 11 || m100 == 12 || m100 == 13 {
            return s + "th";
        }
        match num % 10 {
            1 => s + "st",
            2 => s + "nd",
            3 => s + "rd",
            _ => s + "th",            // includes 0 and ALL negative remainders -1..-9
        }
    }

CRITICAL: the remainders are taken on the SIGNED value, with no abs() anywhere.
Because C/Rust integer remainder truncates toward zero, a negative num yields a
non-positive m100 and a non-positive num%10, so 11/12/13 never match and 1/2/3 never match.
=> EVERY negative number gets the "th" suffix, unconditionally.
   -1 -> "-1th", -2 -> "-2th", -3 -> "-3th", -21 -> "-21th", -101 -> "-101th",
   -2147483648 -> "-2147483648th".
Zero: 0 % 100 == 0, 0 % 10 == 0 -> "0th". "-0" and "+0" both parse to 0 -> "0th".

Positives behave like the normal English rule:
  ...11, ...12, ...13 (mod 100) -> th; else last digit 1->st, 2->nd, 3->rd, else th.
  Verified: 11th 12th 13th 21st 22nd 23rd 42nd 100th 101st 102nd 103rd 111th 112th 113th
            121st 1000th 1001st 1011th 1013th 1111th 1112th 1113th
            2147483641st 2147483642nd 2147483643rd 2147483647th

=== PART C: full line-2 pipeline ===
    line2 = ordinalize(argv_to_num(argv[1]))
Nothing else touches it; argv[2] (the word) is irrelevant to line 2.
If argc <= 2 the program prints nothing at all and exits 0.

